    <h2 style="margin-top:0">When this fits</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>You have an existing site, on Wix, Squarespace, WordPress, GoDaddy, or built by a freelancer, and you want it replaced with custom code one time rather than rebuilt on a subscription.</li>
      <li>You do not want a recurring monthly relationship right now, and even if you might want one later, you do not want it to be a condition of getting the rebuild done.</li>
      <li>You want ownership of the source code, the source files, and the Cloudflare Pages deploy itself, with all three transferred to you on launch day.</li>
      <li>You want a fixed price and a fixed timeline, and two weeks at a flat $4,500 lines up with how you would prefer to buy the work.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What you get</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Up to 10 pages</strong>, custom-coded from a blank file. Same architecture as the <a href="/samples/" class="inline-link">six demo sites</a>, Eleventy v3, vanilla CSS, vanilla JavaScript, Cloudflare Pages.</li>
      <li><strong>Real custom design.</strong> Not a template. Each project gets a unique visual treatment within the structure of the <a href="/method/" class="inline-link">Service Site Standard</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>95&ndash;100 mobile PageSpeed</strong> on every page. WCAG 2.2 AA accessibility. LocalBusiness + Service schema custom-written for your business.</li>
      <li><strong>One service-area map</strong> via Leaflet + OpenStreetMap, locked to your service area.</li>
      <li><strong>Full handoff package:</strong> source code in your GitHub org, Cloudflare Pages deploy ownership transferred to your account, all assets, all DNS records documented.</li>
      <li><strong>30-day post-launch support</strong> for any bugs or launch-day issues at no extra cost.</li>
      <li><strong>Optional $50/month hosting</strong> if you want me to keep running Cloudflare and email forwarding for you. Pure pass-through; cancel any time, take the site to any host.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What you do not get (vs the monthly plan)</h2>
    <p>The Rebuild Sprint is a one-shot product. It does not include:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Unlimited content updates after the 30-day post-launch window.</li>
      <li>Ongoing performance / accessibility / security audits.</li>
      <li>Quarterly content refreshes.</li>
      <li>Priority response on edit requests.</li>
      <li>Future page additions at no extra cost.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>If any of those matter to you, the <a href="/pricing/" class="inline-link">monthly plan ($175/mo)</a> is the better fit. If you want the rebuild and then to manage the site yourself or with a friend who can edit HTML, the Sprint is the right product.</p>

    <h2>Two-week timeline</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Day 0:</strong> 20-minute discovery call. We confirm scope, brand direction, and the page list. The sprint starts shortly after the call ends.</li>
      <li><strong>Days 1&ndash;3:</strong> design preview ships to a private URL. You give feedback once; we iterate.</li>
      <li><strong>Days 4&ndash;9:</strong> build. All 10 pages, schema, forms, service-area map, accessibility audit, Lighthouse runs.</li>
      <li><strong>Days 10&ndash;12:</strong> pre-launch QA. Real-device mobile testing, schema validation, content load, 301 redirects from your old URLs.</li>
      <li><strong>Day 13:</strong> launch. DNS migration, sitemap submission, Search Console setup, post-launch checks.</li>
      <li><strong>Day 14:</strong> handoff. Source code transferred, Cloudflare Pages ownership transferred, runbook PDF emailed. The site is fully yours.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Pricing</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>$4,500 flat.</strong> 50% on signed agreement to start the sprint, 50% on launch.</li>
      <li><strong>Optional $50/month hosting</strong> if you want me to manage Cloudflare and email forwarding. Cancel any time.</li>
      <li><strong>If you graduate to the monthly plan within 90 days,</strong> the Sprint price counts as the first 12 months of the monthly plan ($2,100 credit). You upgrade for $300/year of the difference, no rebuild.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Compared to a traditional agency</h2>
    <p>A traditional regional agency for a similar scope tends to quote between five and fifteen thousand dollars plus an ongoing maintenance retainer. The agency model bundles together the build itself, an ongoing strategic relationship, maintenance, and a layer of account-management overhead, which makes the total cost difficult to evaluate before you sign. The Rebuild Sprint unbundles all of that. You get a real custom site for the build price, with nothing else attached and no surprises later. The <a href="/vs-agencies/" class="inline-link">full agency comparison</a> covers this in more detail.</p>

    <h2>Frequently asked</h2>
    <h3>Can I add pages later?</h3>
    <p>Yes, you can. The two options are to pay per page at $150 each, billed once and usually live within one to three business days, or to sign on for the monthly plan, in which case page additions are included up to a sensible cap.</p>

    <h3>What if I want more than 10 pages from day one?</h3>
    <p>Each additional page beyond ten in the initial sprint adds $200 to the price, billed at signing. Twelve pages would come to $4,900, fifteen pages would come to $5,500, and once you go past twenty pages the monthly plan or a longer engagement is generally the better shape for the work.</p>

    <h3>Is the source code really mine?</h3>
    <p>Yes, it is. The git repository is created inside your GitHub organization, or a personal repo you nominate, and I hold a maintainer credential only during the sprint itself. On launch day that credential is removed and full ownership of the repo passes to you, which means you can fork it, clone it, or hand the codebase to any developer at any time. The posture is the same as the lump-sum plan.</p>

    <h3>What if something breaks after the 30-day post-launch window?</h3>
    <p>I will help, and I will quote the work fairly. Most of the things that go wrong on a static custom-coded site are content-level rather than code-level, and you can usually fix them yourself with a brief markdown edit. For real code issues I bill at $150 per hour with a transparent estimate provided before any work starts.</p>

